    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61854,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61855,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are often high-stage at the time of diagnosis {15139057; 22588065}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61856,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Carcinosarcomas account for approximately 2% of all ovarian malignancies in clinicopathological studies. These tumours typically occur in postmenopausal women aged &gt;&nbsp;60&nbsp;years {15139057; 22588065}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61857,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61858,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are of epithelial cell origin, and molecular studies support a monoclonal origin by revealing concordant <em>TP53</em> abnormalities within the carcinoma and sarcoma components {9496259; 10646862; 14501818}. Further indirect evidence supporting the epithelial origin is that recurrences are usually high-grade serous carcinoma (HGSC) {12068173} and that the metastatic pattern is similar to that of HGSCs.</p>\r\n<p>Ovarian carcinosarcoma frequently harbours the same genetic alterations seen in uterine carcinosarcoma. Deletions of <em>TP53</em> and <em>MBD3</em> have been reported, as has frequent amplification of chromosome segments containing <em>PIK3CA</em>, <em>CCNE1</em>, <em>TERT</em>, and <em>MYC</em>. In addition, an excess of mutations in genes encoding histones H2A and H2B has also been implicated {27791010}. Other reported genes with overexpression or mutations are <em>CDKN1B</em> (p27KIP1), <em>CTNNB1</em>, <em>FBXW7</em> (a tumour suppressor gene previously identified in uterine serous cancer), <em>PPP2R1A</em>, <em>BCOR</em>, and <em>CHD4</em> {28292439; 25233892}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61859,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour is characteristically large (mean size: 14&nbsp;cm) and predominantly solid, with frequent cystic degeneration and extensive haemorrhage and necrosis {22588065}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61860,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are composed of high-grade carcinoma and sarcoma. One or the other may predominate. The two components are distinct but are typically intermingled with one another. The carcinomatous component is most often HGSC, but other histological types may be seen {22588065}. The sarcomatous elements are classified as homologous when the stromal component has a nonspecific appearance or as heterologous when rhabdomyosarcoma (most commonly), chondrosarcoma, or osteosarcoma (or rarely liposarcoma or angiosarcoma) is present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61861,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61862,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61863,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a biphasic pattern with high-grade carcinomatous and sarcomatous elements.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61864,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Carcinosarcoma is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:10:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61865,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 64,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The median survival time is &lt;&nbsp;24&nbsp;months and the 5-year survival rate is 15&ndash;30%, which compare unfavourably with those of HGSC {15139057; 22588065; 17395252; 21420726}. Most tumours are diagnosed at advanced stage (stage&nbsp;III), and optimal tumour debulking is the most important prognostic factor {15139057}. The presence of extraovarian sarcomatous elements is thought to be an adverse prognostic factor {22588065}. Tumours with an epithelial component of HGSC may exhibit <em>BRCA1</em> or <em>BRCA2</em> mutations, with potential therapeutic impact.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:59:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]